## Usage

RuleX draws a vertical rule at `x` across the frame: at a constant or a time (a release date), at each row of a column (one rule per event), or at the pointer (`x="@pointer_x"`). By default it spans the frame's visible domain in y (`"@view_y_min"` to `"@view_y_max"`), so it reaches the frame's edges as the plot pans and zooms; `y0` and `y1` give other ends, numbers or columns.

The rule follows the frame's coordinates: a spoke in a polar frame, a meridian on a map or a globe. On a band axis, `x` is a category. Rules never widen automatic domains.

RuleX is a built-in template, built from transforms and simpler marks: import it from `hmplot/charts`.

<Note title="SVG and PDF export">

**Vector, except** in a 3D view that holds a mark with no vector form there (Points3D, Surface or another 3D mark, a Heatmap or Image, a lit or additive mark, a globe or polar frame): then SVG and PDF exports draw all of that view's marks as one image, and `mode="vector"` refuses it. In other 3D views the rules are vectors too, drawn back to front by depth (approximate where marks cross closely). `dash` and a `color` column stay vector. See [what is not exported as vector](/docs/export).

</Note>

For Plotly users, this is the `add_vline` pattern. For a shaded interval, use [BandX](/reference/svelte/band-x) (the corresponding `add_vrect` pattern).
